  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name then family name. Irish first names come in Irish and English forms (Seán and John, Pádraig and Patrick), and Irish forms like Aoife, Oisín, Saoirse and Tadhg are very common today, written with their fadas. Surnames often carry O' or Mac; in Irish a woman's surname changes form (Ó Súilleabháin for a man, Ní Shúilleabháin for a woman), but in everyday English the anglicised O'Sullivan is the usual one. Dublin also has large Polish, Lithuanian, Romanian, Brazilian, Indian, Nigerian and Filipino communities. Polish surnames change their ending for women, so the list keeps ones that do not.",
